.TH CC 1
.SH NAME
cc \- sicc, the C compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cc
.RB [ \-c | \-S | \-E ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-I \fIdir\fR]
.RB [ \-D \fIname\fR[=\fIvalue\fR]]
.RB [ \-U \fIname\fR]
.RB [ \-O0 | \-O | \-O2 | \-Os ]
.RB [ \-l \fIlib\fR]
.RB [ \-L \fIdir\fR]
.I file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cc
is sicc, SIEOS's own C compiler, built as a SIEOS program: the same
compiler that builds SIEOS on the development machine, for x86-64 and
AArch64. It preprocesses, compiles, assembles and links in one program;
C11 with the GNU extensions SIEOS uses.
.PP
Sources are files ending in
.BR .c ,
.B .S
or
.B .s
(assembly) and
.B .i
(preprocessed); other words are objects or libraries for the link.
Without
.BR \-c ,
.B \-S
or
.BR \-E ,
the result is a SIEOS program linked as SIEOS's own are
.RB ( user.ld ,
.BR crt0.o ,
.BR lib.o ,
.B libsieos.a
and sicc's runtime
.BR rt.o ,
all in
.BR /lib/sicc ),
made executable;
.B a.out
if there is no
.BR \-o .
The driver defines
.B __SIEOS__
and reads its headers in
.BR /lib/sicc/include .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Compile to an object only.
.TP
.B \-S
Assembly text only.
.TP
.B \-E
Preprocessed text only.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
The output's name (one source only with
.BR \-c ,
.B \-S
or
.BR \-E ).
.TP
.BI \-I " dir\fR,\fP " \-isystem " dir"
Look for headers in
.I dir
too.
.TP
.BI \-D " name\fR[\fP=value\fR],\fP " \-U " name"
Define or undefine a macro.
.TP
.BR \-O0 ", " \-O ", " \-O2 ", " \-Os
No optimization, the optimizer, or the optimizer for size
.RB ( \-Oz
too).
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-g0
Debug information on or off.
.TP
.BR \-ffreestanding ", " \-fpic ", " \-mgeneral-regs-only ", " \-mavx2 ", " \-march= \fIarch\fR
Code generation as gcc's options of the same names
.RB ( \-march=
also selects AArch64's LSE atomics and dot products).
.TP
.BI \-l " lib" "\fR, \fP" \-L " dir" "\fR, \fP" \-T " script" "\fR, \fP" \-Wl, args
Passed to the linker, as are
.BR \-nostdlib ,
.B \-static
and
.BR \-r ;
.BR \-nostdlib ,
.B \-T
and
.B \-r
leave out SIEOS's start and library.
.TP
.BI \-\-target= arch
.B x86_64
or
.B aarch64
(default: the machine it runs on).
.PP
Other options
.RB ( \-W ...,
.BR \-std= ...,
.BR \-f ...,
.BR \-m ...)
are accepted and ignored.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/sicc
headers, start files, library and linker script programs are built with.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cc hello.c \-o hello
\&./hello
cc \-O2 \-c a.c
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR make (1),
.BR sh (1)
.SH BUGS
On x86-64,
.B long double
constants are read at
.B double
precision. A program's arguments, with its folder, must fit in 256 bytes
and 31 words.
